From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0401-black-world-02-upscale-stand-in-steady-pair-and-trace.patch

Source: 0401-black-world-02-upscale-stand-in-steady-pair-and-trace.patch
per-dispatch trace (EDEN_NX2_UPSCALE_STEADY, _TRACE)

run75 (T20, Docked, Max Resolution): program 0x1148fc8e00 (first HMMA at 0xc00) was rejected at
91.3 s with no pair, at 106.2 s the stand-in found one ("plain copy") and the world showed clean
and without white for a short time, then it went black again while the program kept being
dispatched. The rejection is remembered (ShaderCache::RethrowIfRejected), so the stand-in runs on
every dispatch; what varies per dispatch is its pair search or its blit, and the log holds no
per-dispatch data: "skipped" and "plain copy" log once per program, the blit line four times
in all, and the TRACE line only when a pair's input changed.

EDEN_NX2_UPSCALE_STEADY=1:
- the program's own handle slots (read, store) that last gave a pair are remembered and tried
  first on every dispatch, before the handle scan;
- a handle of the program's own whose TIC index lies beyond the compute class's TIC limit is
  still read when its TIC entry is mapped and describes a block-linear 2D image of a blit
  format (or E5B9G9R9) with a mapped address, 64..16384 wide: the limit register need not
  follow a TIC pool the game grows. The title-4, Fortune's Weave, Bananza and blind cbuf0
  scans keep the limit.

EDEN_NX2_UPSCALE_TRACE=1 now logs each change of a program's outcome, at most 256 lines:
"<step>: <input> -> <output>, blitted|not accelerated" (step: title 4 slots, Fortune's Weave
slots, remembered slots, program handles, cbuf0 image of the input's size, Bananza slots,
cbuf0 scan), or "no pair (tic limit N, cbuf0 ...): r@0xc0 tic 123 >limit 0x... WxH format F
..." listing the program's own read and store slots as they stand. The next run's log then
says why a dispatch had no pair.

Both unset: nothing changes. Clang syntax check (no warnings) against the T20 tree with
black-world-01. Not built, not run. Which of the candidates (TIC limit, handle contents,
blit refused under memory pressure: run75's VMA heap 1 stood at 6470 of 7069 MiB at 111 s) is
the cause is not known; STEADY covers the first two, the trace names all three.

